Subject: profile: text rasterisation follows the persona, not the host's text engine

`gfx::FontRenderParams` decides how glyphs are rasterised: antialiasing and
its subpixel order, hinting level and autohinter, embedded bitmaps, whether
glyph positions may be fractional, and Skia's text contrast and gamma. The
browser pushes all eight to every renderer as `blink::RendererPreferences`
(third_party/blink/public/common/renderer_preferences/renderer_preferences.h
:36-44), and they land on the glyph coverage bytes a canvas readback
returns, on the gamma correction applied to those bytes, and -- through
subpixel positioning -- on whether text advances are fractional, which
`measureText` and client rects expose without any canvas at all.

Chromium derives them per platform and only per platform. There are three
implementations of `GetFontRenderParams`: Windows asks the shell
(ui/gfx/font_render_params_win.cc), macOS states a constant tuple
(font_render_params_mac.cc), Linux asks the toolkit and then fontconfig
(font_render_params_linux.cc, ui/gtk/gtk_ui.cc,
ui/gfx/linux/fontconfig_util.cc). The last one is the leak: under any
composed persona those values are the HOST's fontconfig and desktop
settings, so a claimed Windows machine on a Linux host rasterises text the
way that particular Linux box was configured to.

So the profile gains the tuple, and the compositor fills it from the
persona. It is a projection rather than an axis, and the distinction is
load-bearing: there is nothing to draw from, because Chromium has exactly
one answer per platform, and a tuple assembled from two platforms' answers
is a machine that does not exist. The `platform-projection` layer in
`--fingerprint-explain` says exactly that -- no draw, no option table, no
host input, nothing a seed can move.

THE TABLE, AND WHERE EVERY VALUE COMES FROM

                        windows   macos     linux
    antialiasing        true      true      true
    subpixel_positioning true     true      false
    autohinter          false     false     true
    use_bitmaps         false     true      true
    hinting             medium    medium    slight
    subpixel_rendering  rgb       rgb       rgb
    text_contrast       1.0       0.0       0.2
    text_gamma          0.0       0.0       1.2

windows -- ui/gfx/font_render_params_win.cc:77-104. The block starts with
antialiasing and subpixel positioning OFF and turns both on when
`SPI_GETFONTSMOOTHING` reports font smoothing enabled, which is the Windows
default; the subpixel order comes from `GetSubpixelRenderingGeometry`
(:31-61, ClearType `PixelStructure` 1 or `FE_FONTSMOOTHINGORIENTATIONRGB` ->
rgb) and is reached only when `SPI_GETFONTSMOOTHINGTYPE` is
`FE_FONTSMOOTHINGCLEARTYPE`, also the default. `autohinter` and
`use_bitmaps` false and `HINTING_MEDIUM` are set outright at :80-82.

macos -- ui/gfx/font_render_params_mac.cc:16-26 states the whole tuple as
"params that match SkiaTextRenderer's default render settings". Note
`use_bitmaps` true, which is macOS alone among the three.

linux -- `autohinter`, `use_bitmaps` and `HINTING_SLIGHT` are the tuple
Chromium's FreeType path calls "the system's default settings"
(ui/gfx/font_render_params_skia.cc:13-26); slight hinting is also what GTK
reports for the near-universal `hintslight` setting (ui/gtk/gtk_ui.cc:
130-131) and what fontconfig's `FC_HINT_SLIGHT` maps to
(ui/gfx/linux/fontconfig_util.cc:150-151); rgb is fontconfig's `rgba` on an
ordinary LCD desktop (:314-317); and `subpixel_positioning` is false because
font_render_params_linux.cc:267 sets it to `device_scale_factor > 1.0f`,
which an unscaled desktop is not.

The two floats are Skia BUILD constants rather than OS queries, so the right
value is what a real Chrome on the claimed OS was compiled with.
skia/BUILD.gn gives `is_win` `SK_GAMMA_SRGB` with `SK_GAMMA_CONTRAST=1.0`
(:137-142), `is_mac` `SK_GAMMA_SRGB` with `0.0` (:126-131), and Linux
`SK_GAMMA_EXPONENT=1.2` with `SK_GAMMA_CONTRAST=0.2` (:113-117);
`SK_GAMMA_SRGB` leaves `SK_GAMMA_EXPONENT` undefined, which
third_party/skia/include/core/SkTypes.h:84-85 defines to be `0.0f`, sRGB.
That config reaches ui/gfx and Blink because `skia_config` is an
`all_dependent_configs` of `//skia` (skia/BUILD.gn:488).

One correction worth stating, because the plan for this patch carried the
other number: Windows is contrast 1.0, not 0.5. The 0.5 in
SkTypes.h:88-93 is the fallback for a build that defines neither macro, and
no Chromium desktop build is such a build. Serving 0.5 while citing "the
Skia macros" would have been this repository's own failure mode -- an
artifact describing something the build does not do.

BOUNDS, AND WHY THE PARSER IS STRICT

`SkSurfaceProps` declares the domain: `kMinContrastInclusive` 0 and
`kMaxContrastInclusive` 1, `kMinGammaInclusive` 0 and `kMaxGammaExclusive` 4
(third_party/skia/include/core/SkSurfaceProps.h:81-84), which is what
ui/gfx/font_util_win.cc:73-85 clamps a registry value into. The schema and
the loader carry the same half-open gamma bound, so 4.0 is refused rather
than clamped -- a value the platform cannot hold is a malformed profile, and
clamping it would serve a number nobody asked for. `config/profile.schema
.json` needed `exclusiveMaximum` for that, which is the first key in the
schema with a half-open bound and the one assertion keyword the reference
resolver's validator did not yet implement.

The loader takes all eight fields or refuses the profile. A partial tuple
leaves the rest to the host, and on Linux "the rest" is the host's
fontconfig -- so a half-written `render_params` would reintroduce exactly
the leak the section exists to close, while looking composed.

Strings rather than the gfx enums for `hinting` and `subpixel_rendering`,
because ui/gfx depends on base and base cannot name
`gfx::FontRenderParams::Hinting`. The vocabulary is the enum member names
lowercased and the parser refuses anything outside it, so the consumer's
mapping is total and never has to invent a value for an unknown string.

WHAT THIS PATCH CONTAINS, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT

The profile half: the schema section, the loader's parse and accessor, the
compositor's per-persona table and projection, the explain line and its new
layer, and the reference resolver's mirror.

Nothing consumes `Profile::font_render_params()` yet. Serving it means
writing `blink::RendererPreferences` from the profile in
chrome/browser/renderer_preferences_util.cc, which is a separate patch, and
what that will and will not reach is worth stating now:
`WebViewImpl::UpdateFontRenderingFromRendererPrefs` is `!IS_MAC` in its
entirety (third_party/blink/renderer/core/exported/web_view_impl.cc:3645),
so on a Mac host the tuple cannot reach Skia through that path at all and
font_platform_data_mac.mm's own flags decide; and DirectWrite glyph masks
are not reachable from a Linux or macOS host under any setting. The ledger
row `fonts.text-render-params` holds that residual, and the composition
limitation for a cross-OS persona already tells an operator that the
surfaces a fork cannot reach stay the host's.

Two implementations, one table: `_FONT_RENDER_PARAMS` in
scripts/profile_resolver.py mirrors `kFontRenderParams` here. The integral
floats are written with a decimal point in C++ and as integers in Python on
purpose -- `base::JSONWriter` with `OPTIONS_OMIT_DOUBLE_TYPE_PRESERVATION`
collapses an integral double to an integer (base/json/json_writer.cc:86-90),
so `1.0` on the Python side would diverge from the compositor's `1`
byte-wise while meaning the same number, and the cross-writer digest in
scripts/test_profile_resolver.py compares bytes.

Verified: `scripts/checkfile.sh` on base/apostate/profile.cc, compose.cc,
compose_unittest.cc and explain.cc; `python3
scripts/check-schema-wiring.py` at zero findings; and the reference
resolver's new test, which pins all three tuples against the citations
above, proves each is seed-invariant across six seeds, and proves the three
are actually different from each other. `base_unittests` was not run --
this wave does not build.

Ledger: fonts.text-render-params, canvas.2d, fonts.text-advances,
emit.browser.renderer-preferences-font-render-params,
emit.blink.web-view-update-font-rendering-from-renderer-prefs,
emit.blink.font-platform-data-create-skfont-mac.

